from pathlib import Path
import csv,html
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
out=root/'docs/reports'
rows=list(csv.DictReader((root/'docs/cloudflare-usage.csv').open()))
pages=[]
def page(title,kicker,body):
 n=len(pages)+1
 pages.append(f'<section class="page"><header>THREEWOOD <span>ENGINEERING INCIDENT REVIEW · 07 NOV 2026</span></header><div class="kicker">{kicker}</div><h1>{title}</h1>{body}<footer><span>Internal review · USD throughout · verified 07 Nov 2026, 19:59 Singapore</span><b>{n:02}</b></footer></section>')
def table(headers,rows):return '<table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+x+'</th>' for x in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+x+'</td>' for x in r)+'</tr>' for r in rows)+'</tbody></table>'
page('Multiplayer billing incident','01 / Executive assessment', '''
<p class="lead">An unattended room service kept scheduling paid background work after players had disconnected. The service is now parked. Its Durable Object namespace and stored records still exist.</p>
<div class="stats"><div><strong>$42.10</strong><span>Durable Object incident charges¹</span></div><div><strong>$89.21</strong><span>Entire supplied invoice</span></div><div><strong>0 reported</strong><span>Room invocations after shutdown²</span></div></div>
<div class="callout"><h2>Direct answer: do we still have Durable Objects?</h2><p><b>Yes.</b> Cloudflare currently lists three namespaces: ThreeWood’s RoomObject and two SofaPaint UsageBudget namespaces. We disabled the ThreeWood service; we did not delete its namespace or stored data. No equivalent shutdown was performed on the other applications.</p></div>
<h2>What is confirmed now</h2><ul><li><b>Solo game:</b> served by Cloudflare Pages project <code>threewood</code>, with no Pages Functions in the production deployment.</li><li><b>Multiplayer:</b> disabled in code and configuration. The custom Worker domain is removed; workers.dev and preview URLs are disabled.</li><li><b>Namespace:</b> <code>threewood-rooms_RoomObject</code> remains provisioned. Retained storage size is unverified.</li><li><b>Other invoice spend:</b> $42.05 intentional Workers AI usage, $5 subscription and $0.06 R2 storage.</li></ul>
<h2>Assessment</h2><p>The sustained expense is strongly attributable to autonomous room alarms, not sustained player demand. The exact per-object execution history cannot be reconstructed because historical request traces were unavailable. Source inspection and retained metrics support the failure mechanism described in this report.</p>
<p class="note">¹ Invoice lines are account-wide; namespace analytics overwhelmingly attribute the Durable Object activity to ThreeWood. ² GraphQL returned no rows since 11:34 UTC / 19:34 Singapore, checked at 11:59 UTC. Reporting can lag. This is point-in-time evidence, not a continuous monitor or a per-object alarm audit.</p>
''')
page('How multiplayer is wired','02 / Intended architecture', '''
<p class="lead">The browser runs the golf game. A separate Worker routes each room to one stateful Durable Object, which coordinates players and runs bots.</p>
<div class="pipeline"><div><b>Cloudflare Pages</b><small>threewood.app · HTML, JavaScript, assets<br>Static production; no Functions</small></div><em>↓ loads into the player’s browser</em><div><b>Browser game + Room client</b><small>src/net/room.js → src/net/netplay.js<br>Local human gameplay, rendering and network events</small></div><em>↓ HTTPS /new · WebSocket /room/ABCD</em><div><b>threewood-rooms Worker</b><small>server/room.js · ROOMS binding<br>idFromName(code) → object stub → fetch()</small></div><em>↓ one object identity per room code</em><div><b>RoomObject</b><small>Roster, host handover, relay, round metadata, bots<br>Hibernatable WebSockets + SQLite-backed storage + alarms</small></div><em>↔ messages broadcast to the room’s browsers</em></div>
<div class="columns"><div><h2>Create / join</h2><p><code>/new</code> returns a random four-letter code; it does not reserve a room. The host connects with <code>?create=1</code>. Joining uses the same code without creation. The object accepts a WebSocket, then processes a <code>hello</code> message and returns welcome/roster data.</p></div><div><h2>Cost boundary</h2><p>Pages asset requests are free. Worker requests, object invocations, active object duration, database writes and stored data are separate metered resources. An alarm can incur cost even when nobody opens the website.</p></div></div>
<p class="note">This is the intended enabled pipeline. Public access to the Worker shown above is currently disconnected. Repository configuration still retains the ROOMS binding and migration history.</p>
''')
page('Room lifecycle and failure mechanism','03 / Technical root cause', '''
<h2>Normal responsibilities</h2><p>Each room supports up to eight seats. Phones ping every five seconds. The server checks quiet sockets on a nominal 15-second sweep; disconnected seats should expire after three minutes. Empty rooms should be forgotten after 60 seconds. Bots step on alarms, nominally every 200 ms while playing.</p>
<p>Saved room state includes the roster, host, creation time and round metadata. The relay also keeps a bounded in-memory log for reconnects. That log and its message sequence are not durable across eviction; reconnect replay is therefore best effort, not a durable exactly-once guarantee. The server is a relay and bot host, not a fully authoritative anti-cheat simulation of human shots.</p>
<h2>Two lifecycle deadlines lived in the wrong place</h2>
''' + table(['Defect','Original behavior','Consequence'],[
['Disconnected seat expiry','away() saved the away timestamp, then the sweep set lostAt only in memory. Expiry compared against lostAt.','Eviction could reset the removal countdown, allowing stale human seats to persist.'],
['Empty-room cleanup','emptySince was set in memory and a wake-up scheduled 60 seconds later; the timestamp was not saved.','A cold constructor could forget the countdown and schedule another 60-second wait.'],
['Autonomous bots','Bot scheduling did not require an attached human socket; no hard room lifetime existed.','Stale rounds could continue background work without a player.']]) + '''
<div class="callout"><h2>The recurring-work loop</h2><p>Alarm wakes room → room restores incomplete lifecycle state → stale seat or empty-room deadline fails to advance to deletion → work or cleanup is scheduled again → storage and compute are billed → repeat.</p></div>
<p>Hibernation/eviction discards JavaScript fields; it does not discard persisted room records or scheduled alarms. The design relied on process memory to decide when persistent work should stop. The ability to sleep between events did not bound the number of future events.</p>
<h2>What we can and cannot prove</h2><p>Both deadline defects are present in the retrieved deployed source. Metrics identify alarm traffic continuing without recorded sockets. This strongly supports the combined mechanism. Missing historical traces mean we cannot determine the exact share of cost caused by each defect, which object first entered the loop, or whether a specific eviction triggered each recurrence.</p>
''')
bills=[('Durable Object rows written','17.00'),('Durable Object compute duration','12.50'),('Durable Object compute requests','12.60'),('Workers AI regular neurons','42.05'),('Workers Paid subscription','5.00'),('R2 data storage','0.06')]
bar=''.join(f'<div class="billbar"><span>{label}</span><div style="width:{float(value)/42.05*220:.1f}px;background:{"#be493b" if i<3 else "#23756b"}"></div><b>${value}</b></div>' for i,(label,value) in enumerate(bills))
page('Where every dollar went','04 / Financial reconciliation', '''
<p class="lead">The full invoice was $89.21. The unexpected Durable Object subtotal was $42.10. Nearly half the bill was intentional image generation, confirmed by the owner.</p>
''' +bar+table(['Invoice component','Charge','Disposition'],[
['Durable Objects: writes + duration + requests','$42.10','Unexpected; room service parked'],['Workers AI','$42.05','Owner confirmed intentional Flux image generation'],['Workers Paid','$5.00','Shared account subscription; retained'],['R2 storage','$0.06','Stored data; retained'],['Total','$89.21','All nonzero lines reconciled']])+'''
<h2>Invoice scope and rounding</h2><p>Invoice <b>IN-87619432</b>, issued November 7, covers metered usage October 7–November 6. The $5 subscription covers November 7–December 6. It lists 83,378,322 excess object requests, 16,401,872 excess written rows and 720,595 excess GB-seconds. Included allocations and billing-unit rounding explain why raw usage multiplied by the displayed unit price does not always equal the charged line exactly. [1]</p>
<h2>Other charges were checked</h2><p>All other lines on the supplied seven-page invoice are $0.00: ordinary Workers requests/CPU, KV, D1, logs, queues, containers, Images and the other listed products. A zero-dollar “Enabled” product is not an additional nonzero subscription on this invoice.</p>
<p>AI analytics show 443 Flux 2 Dev REST calls and about 4.127 million neurons during the billing period; voice-model usage was negligible. Latest recorded AI calls were November 4 at 04:00 UTC. The client or credential that made the REST calls was not identified; no AI credentials were revoked.</p>
<p class="note">The invoice is the source of truth for amounts. GraphQL figures elsewhere are operational aggregates and may be adaptively sampled. Account billing APIs returned 403, so separate invoices or unrelated subscriptions were not exhaustively excluded.</p>
''')
def chart(col,maxval,color):
 return '<div class="daily">'+''.join(f'<div title="{r["date_utc"]}: {r[col]}" style="height:{max(1,float(r[col])/maxval*100):.1f}px;background:{color}"></div>' for r in rows)+'</div><div class="axis"><span>Oct 12</span><span>Oct 24</span><span>Nov 7*</span></div>'
page('Evidence: alarms, not an audience','05 / Impact and detection', '''
<h2>Room alarm invocations per day <span class="unit">scale: 0–3.5 million</span></h2>'''+chart('room_alarm_invocations',3500000,'#be493b')+'''
<h2>Zone page views per day <span class="unit">scale: 0–2,000</span></h2>'''+chart('zone_page_views',2000,'#23756b')+'''
<p class="note">*November 7 is partial, through shutdown. UTC dates. Different vertical scales; charts are intended to compare timing, not bar heights between panels.</p>
''' +table(['Metric / observation','Reported result'],[
['Oct 12–Nov 7 room events','86,032,282 alarms; 130 HTTP; 13,904 hibernation events'],['Room database work','67,695,359 rows written; 14,453,307 rows read'],['Nov 6 contrast','3,433,622 alarms versus 9 outer Worker requests'],['Recorded connections','Maximum active WebSockets was zero on each day Oct 16 onward'],['Nov 6 onward object grouping','102 returned objects; only alarm events in that interval'],['Zone traffic Oct 12–Nov 7','18,158 page views; 29,794 HTTP requests']])+'''
<h2>What the “10k page views” notification meant</h2><p>It was a Cloudflare traffic milestone, not a spending limit or a count of players. Traffic included WordPress probes, unrelated asset paths and a self-identifying scanner. Human traffic may also be present. Solo play happens entirely in the browser, so the audited server metrics cannot measure round starts, completed holes or return visits.</p>
<h2>Detection gap</h2><p>The bill and owner-provided dashboard screenshot triggered investigation. There was no verified room-budget shutdown, lifecycle alert or monitor for “alarm work with no players.” Historical invocation logs/stacks were inaccessible; original settings did not show observability enabled and Logpush was off.</p>
''')
page('Response and validation','06 / Timeline and deployed controls', '''
''' +table(['Time / period','Event and confidence'],[
['Oct 12, 2026','Worker creation date; retained metrics already show alarm activity that day. Exact first defective invocation is unknown.'],['Oct 12–15','Some HTTP/WebSocket-related activity is recorded; it may include development testing.'],['Oct 16–Nov 6','Recurring alarms continue while periodic metrics report no active sockets.'],['Nov 7, 11:33 UTC<br>19:33 Singapore','Shutdown deployment reaches production; invocations fall during this minute.'],['Nov 7, from 11:34 UTC','Subsequent room-namespace queries return no invocation rows.'],['Nov 7, 11:34–11:59 UTC<br>approximate response window','Lifecycle fixes, regression coverage, route removal, invoice investigation and hosting verification completed.'],['Nov 7, 11:59 UTC<br>19:59 Singapore','Fresh API verification confirms retained namespaces, empty Worker-domain list and disabled development/preview endpoints.']])+'''
<h2>Controls actually deployed</h2><ul><li><b>Fail closed:</b> ROOMS_ENABLED must be exactly “true.” Missing or false configuration stops ingress and object work.</li><li><b>Existing work:</b> constructors, alarms and messages cancel alarms and close sockets when disabled; close/error cleanup cannot rearm disabled rooms.</li><li><b>Durable deadlines:</b> emptySince is persisted; disconnected seats use persisted away timestamps.</li><li><b>Bounded work:</b> two-hour maximum room age checked on events; bot ticking requires an attached identified socket.</li><li><b>Cleanup:</b> deleteAlarm() is explicit before deleting stored room state. The compatibility date is January 1, 2026. [2]</li><li><b>Exposure reduction:</b> no custom Worker domain, workers.dev disabled, preview URLs disabled, routes empty.</li><li><b>Invocation ceiling:</b> 50 ms CPU limit configured. This is not a dollar cap.</li></ul>
<h2>Verification performed</h2><p>All 30 tests passed after the first fix set. Six targeted billing tests passed after the second deadline fix: disabled/missing flags, cancellation without rearm, cleanup across simulated reconstruction, maximum lifetime, bot pause and stale-seat expiry. Configuration and public responses were checked. No real Cloudflare hibernation integration test has yet been completed.</p>
<p class="note">Code and documentation are pushed to feat/mobile-18-holes. Worker production was deployed directly; the main checkout contains unrelated edits and was not merged. Pages frontend was not redeployed during containment. Final parked Worker version: 053a538f-d14c-46ea-95c9-e4e890920c22.</p>
''')
page('What remains—and what must change','07 / Prevention and ownership', '''
''' +table(['Action','State','Acceptance / responsibility'],[
['Stop the unattended room loop','Deployed','No reported invocations after shutdown; recheck before any restore.'],['Remove public multiplayer ingress','Verified','No Worker domain; development and preview flags false.'],['Fix transient lifecycle deadlines','Deployed + unit tested','Engineering must still validate actual platform eviction.'],['Delete ThreeWood namespace/data','Not performed','Owner decision; irreversible room-data removal needs a deliberate migration.'],['Account-wide dollar spending cap','Not implemented','No claim of universal spend protection. Shared apps remain active.'],['Admission and message abuse controls','Not completed','Required before public multiplayer launch.'],['Usage budget / automatic cutoff','Not completed','Must bound per-room and aggregate work, including alarms.'],['Lifecycle telemetry and alerts','Not completed','Alert on orphaned rooms, alarm rate, row writes and absent players.'],['Real-player engagement telemetry','Not implemented','Optional anonymous round-start/hole/round-completion counters.']])+'''
<h2>Safe restart criteria</h2><ol><li>Use a separate staging namespace. Exercise disconnects, mobile suspension, eviction, deploys, stale sockets and empty rooms with real Cloudflare execution.</li><li>Show that saved deadlines survive reconstruction, no bot work continues without a genuinely live human, and expired/empty objects stop scheduling alarms.</li><li>Set conservative room-creation, join, socket, message-size and message-rate limits. Validate room-code collisions and admission before using short public codes.</li><li>Implement an aggregate usage budget with a fail-closed cutoff; estimate worst-case bot work. An external alert alone does not enforce that budget.</li><li>Prefer event-driven or client-hosted bots where feasible; reassess the 200 ms autonomous alarm design. Tune the 50 ms CPU limit using measured staging behavior.</li><li>Make restoring the public domain and enabling the flag an explicit reviewed release. Verify the kill switch under load first.</li></ol>
<p class="note">The current bot guard checks for a socket with an attached player ID. That is stronger than the old code but is not itself proof that a human is actively playing. Likewise, unit tests do not prove production hibernation behavior. The implemented fixes reduce risk; keeping the service parked is the current operational protection.</p>
''')
page('Parked-state runbook and references','08 / Handoff', '''
<h2>Keep while the game is inactive</h2><p>Leave Pages serving solo play. Keep <code>ROOMS_ENABLED="false"</code>, <code>workers_dev=false</code>, <code>preview_urls=false</code> and <code>routes=[]</code>. Do not reattach rooms.threewood.app. Check the Cloudflare control plane as well as Wrangler: deployment with an empty route list did not automatically remove the old custom domain; it was explicitly removed through the API.</p>
<h2>Retained resources and future charges</h2><p>Three namespaces remain: <b>threewood-rooms_RoomObject</b>, <b>sofapaint-mcp_UsageBudget</b> and <b>sofapaint-auto_UsageBudget</b>. The room namespace ID is <code>4e52c2a9cb33472b97dcff9d1f287913</code>. Provisioned identity is not proof of current execution; stored data can still be metered. SQL storage on the supplied invoice was $0.00, but exact current stored size was not returned.</p>
<p>The shared account has 11 Worker scripts, five R2 buckets and two KV namespaces; no D1 databases or queues were listed. Daily promote-feedback and hourly sofapaint-mcp jobs remain intact. No comparable second alarm loop was found. Do not delete unrelated resources or downgrade the shared plan solely to park this game. Intentional AI requests can still incur future charges.</p>
<h2>If charges or activity reappear</h2><ol><li>Check the invoice period first: delayed billing for pre-shutdown usage is not new execution.</li><li>Query RoomObject invocations grouped by time and type; inspect Worker domain/subdomain flags and deployed variables.</li><li>If new room work exists, keep ingress disconnected and inspect the live deployed version and alarm path. Avoid a frontend rollback that restores backend routes.</li><li>Reconcile new costs by product and namespace. Escalate with invoice and usage evidence; any billing credit is Cloudflare’s decision.</li></ol>
<h2>Evidence and source register</h2><p class="refs"><b>Primary evidence:</b> supplied invoice IN-87619432 (7 pages); authenticated Cloudflare namespace/domain/subdomain and Pages APIs; GraphQL invocation, periodic, AI and zone analytics; retrieved pre-containment Worker bundle; server/room.js, src/net/room.js and tests/room-billing.test.js.<br><b>Repository records:</b> docs/cloudflare-billing-incident.md; docs/cloudflare-usage.csv. Raw investigation captures remain in /tmp/threewood-incident and should be archived securely if long-term evidence is required; /tmp is not durable storage.<br><b>[1] Durable Object pricing:</b> <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing/">developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing/</a><br><b>[2] Alarms and lifecycle:</b> <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/alarms/">developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/alarms/</a><br><b>[3] Pages static pricing:</b> <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/functions/pricing/">developers.cloudflare.com/pages/functions/pricing/</a></p>
<p class="note">Corrections to early investigation: production hosting is Cloudflare Pages, not legacy Vercel; the entire $89.21 bill was not caused by the game; objects were disabled, not deleted; lack of multiplayer activity does not establish lack of solo play. This report supersedes those earlier ambiguities. Prepared from evidence available November 7, 2026; no continuous monitoring is implied.</p>
''')
